"""Generate the P0 engineering report only from persisted ablation results."""
from core import *


def generate(summary=None):
    s=summary or read(OUT/'geometry_p0/e_coli_core/summary.json')
    rows={r['condition']:r for r in s['results']}
    names={'legacy_replay':'Original implementation replay','scaled_hard':'Physical scale only','display_soft':'Joint display-copy solve','label_aware':'Plus label avoidance'}
    table='\n'.join('| '+names[k]+' | '+' | '.join(str(r[x]) for x in ['node_overlap_pairs','label_overlap_pairs','label_node_intersections','straight_crossings'])+' | '+str(r['routing']['metrics']['proper_crossings'])+' | '+str(r['segment_label_intersections'])+' | '+f"{r['canvas_area_per_reaction']:.0f}"+' |' for k,r in rows.items())
    views='\n'.join('| '+r['view']+' | '+names[r['condition']]+' | '+str(r['reactions'])+' | '+str(r['node_overlap_pairs'])+' | '+str(r['label_overlap_pairs'])+' | '+str(r['straight_crossings'])+' |' for r in s['fixed_view_results'])
    a=rows['legacy_replay'];b=rows['label_aware'];g=rows['scaled_hard'];z=rows['display_soft']
    ratio=b['canvas_area_per_reaction']/a['canvas_area_per_reaction']
    solver=z['constraints']
    text=f'''# P0 results: corrected module scale, graphical copies and constraint handling

Date: 2026-11-06. Generated by `geometry_report.py` from saved results. This round carries out the P0 engineering phase of the [08 plan](08_next_version_plan.md); it connects no new MicroMap templates, retrains no learner, and does not promote single-model results to cross-species conclusions.

## 1. What this round completed

- Modules are no longer limited to normalized coordinates multiplied by 100; an optional physical scale combines the target core edge length, graphical spacing and the label footprint at the final font size. Scaling preserves the relative shape of the expert layout; a finite upper bound cannot guarantee that every template is already conflict-free.
- A single graphical-instance representation was built: reactions, main-participant copies and side participants enter the subsequent geometric solve together. Export consumes the solved graphical coordinates directly and no longer overwrites copies from a separate set of module coordinates.
- Hard/soft anchors are explicit: hard fixes must hold, while retrieval hints use a confidence and a bounded movement range; connection vectors inside a module carry a shape penalty. The current allowed range is a rectangle of +/-120 pixels per axis, not a rotation-invariant radius constraint.
- Bounded optimization and spacing projection handle graphical conflicts. The shape term at this stage depends on the orientation of the selected module and is not claimed to be a complete rotation-equivariant learning algorithm.
- Label selection checks other labels and nodes at the same time and applies a deterministic local adjustment; an independent label-rectangle obstacle interface was added on the same pinned libavoid source commit.

The new capability is run explicitly through the `geometry` command; the original v0.3 model, module library, training results and full comparison are kept. The default `models` still runs the original experiment flow; one engineering case has not been automatically promoted to a new default for all models.

## 2. Experimental control

The full target is E. coli core with 95 reactions, 72 metabolites and 360 reaction-participant associations. The existing 11 modules and their node mappings are fixed: 70 module reactions, 2 bridges and 23 reactions to be completed. The four conditions use the same graphical IDs, participant mapping and copy policy; the graphical graph hash is `{s['display_graph_hash']}`. Increasing coverage and improving geometry are not conflated.

All conditions use the same 12 px font and a 4 px node radius, with random seed {s['seed']}. The node-overlap metric keeps the count of proximity pairs whose centre distance is below 10 px; it excludes routing control points. Label/node overlap is measured from the actual font rectangles and node extents. The font is not shrunk when the canvas grows.

"Edge segments crossing labels" counts segment-label intersections on the final straight/polyline paths, excluding the labels at the segment's own endpoints; it is not a count of independent reactions or independent samples. Straight-line crossings and orthogonal-routing crossings are reported separately. The copy policies of the original v0.3 generic method and the module method differ, so this round relies mainly on ablations under the same graphical policy and does not simply treat the two as the same graphical graph.

## 3. Full-model results

| Condition | Node proximity pairs | Label overlap pairs | Label/node overlaps | Straight crossings | Orthogonal crossings | Segments crossing labels | Canvas area/reaction (px²) |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
{table}

Compared with the original implementation, the final condition reduces node proximity pairs from {a['node_overlap_pairs']} to {b['node_overlap_pairs']}, label overlaps from {a['label_overlap_pairs']} to {b['label_overlap_pairs']}, and straight crossings from {a['straight_crossings']} to {b['straight_crossings']}. But the canvas area is about **{ratio:.2f} times** that of the original implementation. Part of this gain comes from correcting a physical size that was originally too small and cannot be attributed wholly to the new solver.

At the already enlarged physical scale, joint solving still reduces node proximity pairs from {g['node_overlap_pairs']} to {z['node_overlap_pairs']} and canvas area/reaction from {g['canvas_area_per_reaction']:.0f} to {z['canvas_area_per_reaction']:.0f}; enlarging the canvas is therefore not its only effect.

Label avoidance reduces edge segments crossing labels from {z['segment_label_intersections']} to {b['segment_label_intersections']}, but orthogonal crossings change from {z['routing']['metrics']['proper_crossings']} to {b['routing']['metrics']['proper_crossings']} and bends from {z['routing']['metrics']['bends']} to {b['routing']['metrics']['bends']}. This trade-off must be kept; it cannot be described as an improvement in every readability metric.

## 4. Fixed-subgraph results

The three existing target scopes are reused and solved separately under the same participant/copy policy; all subgraphs are exported with straight lines to avoid mixing in different routing conditions. They come from a single model and do not constitute three independent species or three independent expert sources.

| Scope | Condition | Reactions | Node proximity pairs | Label overlap pairs | Straight crossings |
|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
{views}

Node proximity and label conflicts are listed independently for each scope so that a whole-graph average does not hide local regressions; for example, joint solving is not necessarily better than "physical scale only" on the crossing metric in every subgraph.

## 5. Constraints and validity

All exports pass the Escher schema, with participants, stoichiometry and the original-model hash checked reaction by reaction. The default retrieval graph has no user hard-fixed nodes, so zero hard-anchor drift in the actual case is not itself test evidence; separate tests cover hard/soft mixing, fixed conflicts being kept and reported, movement ranges, and coordinate propagation to graphical copies.

The whole-graph joint solve has {solver['remaining_spacing_violations']} spacing violations, a module-internal connection-vector RMSE of {solver['shape_vector_rmse']:.2f} px and a maximum hint displacement of {solver['hint_max_drift']:.2f} px. The optimizer convergence flag is `{solver['optimizer_converged']}` with the message `{solver['optimizer_message']}`. After the 350-step budget it returned geometry that satisfies the checked spacing and anchor bounds, but it cannot be claimed to have converged or to be globally optimal.

There are still {b['label_overlap_pairs']} label overlap pairs, {b['label_node_intersections']} label/node overlaps and {b['segment_label_intersections']} edge segments crossing labels; the whole graph is still spread out, energy-related regions are still crowded, and real membrane boundaries are not yet implemented. Block sizes are currently estimated together with the label footprint and have not been used to rearrange ELK from feedback on the final side metabolites and label boundaries. The results of this round are evidence of engineering improvement, not of publication-grade automatic drawing or of learning gain.

## 6. Files and rerun

```sh
python3 project.py geometry --model e_coli_core
python3 project.py verify
```

- [Entry point of the four-condition comparison](../layout_atlas/results/geometry_p0/e_coli_core/index.html)
- [Final SVG](../layout_atlas/results/geometry_p0/e_coli_core/label_aware/map.svg) / [Escher JSON](../layout_atlas/results/geometry_p0/e_coli_core/label_aware/map.json)
- [Full per-condition and subgraph metrics](../layout_atlas/results/geometry_p0/e_coli_core/summary.json)
- Each condition's `scene.json` stores the graphical IDs, the reaction-participant-glyph mapping and the original solved coordinates; the joint-solve condition also contains the actual soft/hard anchor contract. Export applies one uniform canvas translation to fit the labels; this translation does not change relative geometry.
- The summary records the target, the candidate set, the skeleton files and the code SHA256; the configuration in the source is this round's fixed configuration, not an unvalidated command-line tuning interface.

## 7. Decisions for follow-up work

P0 supports continuing on one complete small model and its fixed sub-scopes: scale and graphical consistency are indeed engineering problems to be solved. The explicit experimental conditions are kept; validation on fixed scopes of another species comes first, and replacing all production exports is considered only afterwards.

The next priority is P1 of the 08 plan: reaction-level identity bridging, real CellDesigner path decoding and an independent MicroMap extension package. The same corrected geometry conditions must then be used to compare "current library" with "current library + MicroMap", and the P0 drawing improvements must not be passed off as a contribution of the new data source. Learned models get a separate paired experiment once the graphical and anchor contract is unified.
'''
    path=ROOT/'Proposal/09_p0_geometry_results.md';path.write_text(text)
    return path

if __name__=='__main__':print(generate())
